.. front-page content, minus the title, which index.rst supplies. Mirrors
   FINUFFT's docs/overview.src.

`treeweave <https://github.com/DiamonDinoia/treeweave>`_ replaces a costly
function ``f`` on a box by a piecewise polynomial that evaluates in a few
nanoseconds and agrees with ``f`` to a tolerance the caller picks. The fit is
automatic: treeweave subdivides the box until every panel meets the tolerance,
so the caller supplies ``f``, the corners and ``tol``, and nothing else. It
works in **one, two and three dimensions from every language** (C, C++,
Fortran, Python, Julia, MATLAB/Octave, JavaScript/TypeScript) and in
**arbitrary dimension from C++**, where the dimension is a template parameter.
Single and double precision are both supported, scalar and vector valued ``f``
alike. A fitted object is immutable and its evaluators are thread-safe. The
library is C++20, gets its portable SIMD from
`xsimd <https://github.com/xtensor-stack/xsimd>`_ and
`POET <https://github.com/flatironinstitute/poet>`_, has no runtime dependency
beyond libm, and is `BSD-3-Clause licensed
<https://github.com/DiamonDinoia/treeweave/blob/main/LICENSE>`_. It is developed
at the `Center for Computational Mathematics
<https://www.simonsfoundation.org/flatiron/center-for-computational-mathematics/>`_
of the `Flatiron Institute <https://www.simonsfoundation.org/flatiron>`_ and
succeeds `baobzi <https://github.com/flatironinstitute/baobzi>`_.

Jump to :doc:`install` for the shortest working example in your language.

.. _what:

What does treeweave do?
-----------------------

Given :math:`f : [a, b]^d \to \mathbb{R}^m` and a tolerance :math:`\varepsilon`, treeweave
builds an approximant :math:`\tilde f` with

.. math:: \max_{x \in [a,b]^d} \lVert \tilde f(x) - f(x) \rVert \;\le\; \varepsilon \max_{x} \lVert f(x) \rVert ~,
   :label: goal

and the program then evaluates :math:`\tilde f` wherever it used to call :math:`f`.
:math:`\tilde f` is a *piecewise* polynomial. The box is cut into panels; each panel
carries one fixed-degree tensor-product polynomial fitted at Chebyshev nodes.
One evaluation is a tree descent to the panel containing :math:`x`, then a Horner
sweep over that panel's :math:`(\text{degree}+1)^d` coefficients. Neither step calls
:math:`f`.

The panels are **not** a uniform grid. treeweave starts with the whole box,
tries a fit, and splits any panel whose measured error exceeds :math:`\varepsilon`.
That is the entire algorithm, and it is what makes the accuracy request
meaningful: the caller asks for :math:`\varepsilon` and gets :math:`\varepsilon`, with no
grid, degree, knot vector or smoothing parameter to choose.

The picture in one dimension
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

``examples/c++/panels1d.cpp`` fits :math:`f(x) = 1/(x - 1.05)` on :math:`[-1, 1]`: smooth
everywhere on the domain, but with a pole just past the right end, so its
derivatives blow up as :math:`x \to 1`. The program recovers the panel boundaries
through the public API alone and prints them. This is its actual output,
captured when these docs were built:

.. literalinclude:: _generated/panels1d.txt
   :language: text

Panels stay coarse on the left, where :math:`f` is nearly flat, and halve repeatedly
toward the right, where it is not. That is adaptive paneling, and it is the one
picture worth carrying: **refinement buys accuracy only where accuracy is
expensive.** The source is short:

.. literalinclude:: ../examples/c++/panels1d.cpp
   :language: cpp

:doc:`how-treeweave-works` covers the same construction in 2-D and 3-D, where a
split makes 4 or 8 children instead of 2, and gives the numerical analysis and
the references.

.. _sings:

Singularities: what happens, and what to do
-------------------------------------------

A polynomial is smooth everywhere. The function you want to fit may not be.
Everything interesting about treeweave's behaviour follows from how far the
trouble is from the domain, and ``examples/c++/singularity.cpp`` walks the three
cases in order. This is its output:

.. literalinclude:: _generated/singularity.txt
   :language: text

Read it as a story in three acts.

**Act one: the pole is outside the domain, and adaptivity just works.** Move the
pole from a distance of 1 down to 1e-4 and the panel count goes 11, 33, 56, 103.
Ten times closer costs roughly twenty more panels, not ten times more, because
halving a panel halves its width while its distance to the pole barely changes,
and the Chebyshev error on that panel drops geometrically. The cost is the
*logarithm* of the distance. This is the case almost every real function is in:
a Bessel function near its argument's origin, a Green's function near contact, a
partition function near a phase boundary. You do nothing, and it works.

That act has an ending, though. At a gap of 1e-8 the fit stops and throws
``MemoryBudgetExceeded``. The logarithm is cheap but it is not free, and the
default budget in one dimension is 4 MiB. Raise ``max_memory_mib`` if the memory
is worth it to you; the point is that treeweave makes you decide, rather than
deciding quietly on your behalf.

**Act two: the pole is on the boundary, and refinement never terminates.** The
panel touching :math:`x = 1` contains the singularity no matter how small it
gets, so its error never falls below the tolerance and the tree keeps splitting
until a limit stops it. treeweave throws. The fix is one character of arithmetic:
fit on ``[-1, 0.9]`` instead, and the same function needs 33 panels and 5.7 KiB.
``examples/c++/hankel.cpp`` does exactly this, fitting :math:`H_0^{(1)}` on
:math:`[1, 30]` because :math:`Y_0` diverges at the origin.

**Act three: the pole is strictly inside, and no panel layout can help.** Here
treeweave throws ``MaxDepthExceeded``, and the fix is to split the domain at the
singularity and fit each side, which turns act three into two copies of act two:
71 panels and 11.3 KiB each. If the singular factor is known analytically,
better still is to divide it out and fit only the smooth cofactor, which costs
nothing extra at evaluation time.

The thread running through all three acts is that **treeweave never returns a
quietly wrong answer.** A fit either meets the tolerance you asked for or raises.
If you would rather inspect the damage than handle an exception, set
``allow_max_depth_leaves`` and the non-converged panels come back through
``Function::non_converged_panels()``. :doc:`guides/options` documents both knobs.

.. _why:

Why treeweave? Comparison against other interpolation approaches
----------------------------------------------------------------

Adaptive piecewise Chebyshev interpolation is textbook numerical analysis, not
a new idea; :doc:`how-treeweave-works` cites the sources. What treeweave adds
is an error-driven fit wired to an evaluation path written for a vector unit,
behind one API in seven languages.

Here is the whole argument in one table. ``benchmarks/compare_interpolators.py``
fits each target with treeweave and with ``scipy.interpolate.CubicSpline``,
growing the spline's uniform grid until it *meets* the same accuracy, so every
row is measured at the accuracy it achieved:

.. list-table::
   :header-rows: 1
   :widths: 34 10 14 12 14 16

   * - Target, fitted to 1e-10
     - method
     - calls to :math:`f`
     - memory
     - Meval/s
     - achieved
   * - :math:`\zeta(s)`, 1000 terms, on [2, 10]
     - treeweave
     - 601
     - 3.0 KiB
     - 224
     - 5.1e-11
   * -
     - CubicSpline
     - 2048
     - 80 KiB
     - 14
     - 7.7e-11
   * - :math:`1/(x - 1.05)` on [-1, 1]
     - treeweave
     - 1201
     - 5.9 KiB
     - 271
     - 5.5e-11
   * -
     - CubicSpline
     - 16384
     - 640 KiB
     - 11
     - 2.4e-11

108x less memory on the near-singular target, 14x fewer calls to :math:`f`, and
16x to 25x the evaluation throughput. :doc:`guides/performance` runs the same
comparison against sixteen other methods in four languages, and it is where the
cases treeweave loses are written down: on ``sin(30 x)`` at 1e-6, oscillation
with no local structure, a uniform grid is already the right answer and the
spline needs fewer samples, and on any target analytic across the whole domain
a single global Chebyshev series stores less than treeweave does. Where the
numbers come from:

* **The caller states accuracy, not resolution.** ``scipy``'s ``CubicSpline``
  and ``RegularGridInterpolator``, GSL's splines, and every grid-based
  interpolator ask *where* to sample and then deliver whatever error follows
  from that choice. treeweave asks *how accurate* and refines until it gets
  there. There is nothing to tune, and no way to be three digits off because
  one corner of the domain was under-resolved.

* **Exponential, not algebraic, convergence.** A cubic spline's error decays
  like :math:`h^4`, so each extra digit of accuracy costs 1.8x the knots,
  forever. A Chebyshev panel on an analytic :math:`f` converges *exponentially*
  in the degree, which is why treeweave reaches :math:`10^{-14}` at degree 7 on
  a modest number of panels, and why asking a spline for machine precision is
  not a reasonable thing to do.

* **The function is the input, not a table.** ``scipy.interpolate`` and
  ``sklearn`` take samples, which means the caller has already fixed the sample
  locations and absorbed the resulting error. treeweave takes the callable and
  samples it where its own error estimate says to. That is what lets it report
  an error bound at all.

* **Evaluation is built for throughput.** The tree is a flat array of indices;
  a leaf polynomial is a degree-7 monomial, one SIMD lane wide; and the batch
  path counting-sorts query points by panel so each panel's coefficients are
  loaded once and stay hot in L1.

* **One implementation, seven languages.** Every wrapper calls the same C ABI,
  so an accuracy or throughput result carries between languages. A pure-Python
  or pure-Julia adaptive interpolator does not carry over into a C++ solver's
  inner loop.

What treeweave deliberately does not do: extrapolate (out-of-domain input
returns ``NaN``), resolve interior singularities, interpolate scattered data,
fit unbounded domains, or use a GPU. :doc:`related` lists packages that cover
some of that.

.. _need:

Do I even need treeweave?
-------------------------

Two numbers decide it: :math:`C_f`, the cost of one call to :math:`f`, and :math:`N`, how many
evaluations the program makes. The fit costs about
:math:`C_f \times (\text{panels}) \times (\text{degree}+1)^d` once; each evaluation
afterwards costs one tree descent plus :math:`(\text{degree}+1)^d - 1` FMAs.

* **:math:`N` large and :math:`C_f` well above a few nanoseconds** (a special function, a
  quadrature, a series, a small linear solve, a table lookup with
  interpolation): this is the case treeweave is for. For scale,
  ``benchmarks/zeta_bench.cpp`` fits a truncated Riemann-zeta sum on :math:`[2, 10]`
  to :math:`10^{-10}` and measures 147 Mevals/s single-point and 280 Mevals/s on a
  sorted batch, against 0.78 Mevals/s for the sum itself: 188x and 357x
  (one core, x86-64, GCC 15, ``-O3 -march=native``).
* **:math:`f` already cheap**, a handful of arithmetic operations: skip it. Nothing
  beats evaluating :math:`f`.
* **:math:`N` small**, fewer evaluations than the fit's own sample count: skip it.
  The fit will not amortize.
* **:math:`f` changes every call, or the domain is not known ahead of time**: skip it.
  treeweave fits once, over a fixed box.
* **:math:`d \ge 4`**: available from C++, but weigh it first. Leaf storage grows as
  :math:`(\text{degree}+1)^d` per panel and a split makes :math:`2^d` children, so memory,
  fit time and compile time all grow geometrically. Fitting
  :math:`e^{-\lVert x \rVert^2}` on the unit box to :math:`10^{-8}` at the default degree
  measures as:

  .. list-table::
     :header-rows: 1
     :widths: 12 30 30

     * - :math:`d`
       - compile time
       - leaf coefficients
     * - 1
       - 2.0 s
       - 680 B
     * - 2
       - 2.9 s
       - 7.6 KiB
     * - 3
       - 3.9 s
       - 181 KiB
     * - 4
       - 19.1 s
       - 4.9 MiB

  (one translation unit, GCC 15, ``-O2``, x86-64.) Past about :math:`d = 4`, low-rank
  methods such as tensor trains or sparse grids are the better trade.
